# Fundamental Group of the Quotient Space

## Setup

Let $r: \mathbb{S}^1 \to \mathbb{S}^1$ be rotation by $2\pi/n$:
$$r(\cos\theta, \sin\theta) = (\cos(\theta + 2\pi/n),\, \sin(\theta + 2\pi/n)).$$

The equivalence relation identifies $x \sim r(x) \sim r^2(x) \sim \cdots \sim r^{n-1}(x)$ for every $x \in \mathbb{S}^1$. The quotient space is $X = \mathbb{S}^1 /{\sim}$.

## The quotient is homeomorphic to $\mathbb{S}^1$

**Claim.** The map $r$ generates a free action of the cyclic group $G = \mathbb{Z}_n = \{e, r, r^2, \ldots, r^{n-1}\}$ on $\mathbb{S}^1$.

*Proof of claim.* Each $r^k$ ($1 \le k \le n-1$) is rotation by $2\pi k/n$, which is not the identity map. Hence $r^k(x) \neq x$ for all $x$ whenever $1 \le k \le n-1$: the action is free. $\square$

Since $G$ is a finite group acting freely and continuously on the compact Hausdorff space $\mathbb{S}^1$, the quotient map $q: \mathbb{S}^1 \to X = \mathbb{S}^1/G$ is a **covering map** with $|G| = n$ sheets.

**Explicit homeomorphism $X \cong \mathbb{S}^1$.** Define
$$\varphi: \mathbb{S}^1 \to \mathbb{S}^1, \qquad \varphi(\cos\theta,\sin\theta) = (\cos(n\theta),\, \sin(n\theta)).$$

This is the $n$-fold winding map. Observe:
$$\varphi(r(\cos\theta,\sin\theta)) = \varphi(\cos(\theta+2\pi/n),\sin(\theta+2\pi/n)) = (\cos(n\theta+2\pi),\,\sin(n\theta+2\pi)) = \varphi(\cos\theta,\sin\theta).$$

So $\varphi$ is constant on each equivalence class, hence descends to a well-defined continuous map $\bar\varphi: X \to \mathbb{S}^1$ with $\varphi = \bar\varphi \circ q$.

Conversely, define $\psi: \mathbb{S}^1 \to X$ by $\psi(\cos\phi,\sin\phi) = q(\cos(\phi/n),\sin(\phi/n))$. This is well-defined: the choice of $\phi/n$ versus $\phi/n + 2\pi/n$ differs by the action of $r$, which is identified in the quotient. One checks $\bar\varphi$ and $\bar\psi$ are continuous inverses, so $X \cong \mathbb{S}^1$.

## Computing the fundamental group

Since $X \cong \mathbb{S}^1$, we have
$$\pi_1(X) \cong \pi_1(\mathbb{S}^1) \cong \mathbb{Z}.$$

### Verification via covering space theory

The quotient map $q: \mathbb{S}^1 \to X$ is an $n$-sheeted covering. The fundamental group $\pi_1(\mathbb{S}^1) \cong \mathbb{Z}$ fits into the exact sequence of the covering:
$$1 \to \pi_1(\mathbb{S}^1) \xrightarrow{q_*} \pi_1(X) \to G \to 1,$$
i.e.,
$$1 \to \mathbb{Z} \xrightarrow{\times n} \pi_1(X) \to \mathbb{Z}_n \to 1.$$

The generator of $\pi_1(\mathbb{S}^1)$ (one loop around $\mathbb{S}^1$) maps under $q_*$ to a loop that winds once around $X$; since $q$ wraps $\mathbb{S}^1$ onto $X$ $n$-to-$1$, the image $q_*([\text{generator}])$ corresponds to $n$ times the generator of $\pi_1(X) \cong \mathbb{Z}$. This is consistent: the kernel of $\pi_1(X) \to \mathbb{Z}_n$ (reduction mod $n$) is $n\mathbb{Z}$, which is exactly the image of $q_*$.

## Conclusion

The quotient space $X = \mathbb{S}^1/{\sim}$ is homeomorphic to $\mathbb{S}^1$, and its fundamental group is

$$\boxed{\pi_1(X) \cong \mathbb{Z}.}$$

